  "documented_notes": {
    "About": "Main sheet \"Data\": one row per minute, continuous dates and times. This is a semi-synthetic dataset: environmental columns are based on the real logs, soil moisture and the irrigation targets are simulated, and realistic sensor noise, missing values, outliers and label errors were added on purpose (see \"Injected data-quality issues\"). Describe it as semi-synthetic in any publication. The clean version without these issues is irrigation_continuous_corrected_v5.xlsx.",
    "Injected data-quality issues": "Noise: soil moisture +/-1.1 points (slowly wandering), soil temperature +/-0.08 C in 0.0625 C steps (DS18B20), air temperature +/-0.25 C, humidity +/-1.2% as whole numbers (DHT11), LDR +/-1.5. Missing values (blank cells): single cells removed at random rows, independently in each column (no blocks of consecutive rows). Missing per column: Soil Moisture 1 2514 (1.8%), Soil Moisture 2 2514 (1.8%), Soil Temp1 2514 (1.8%), Soil Temp2 2514 (1.8%), Env Temp 2514 (1.8%), Humidity 2514 (1.8%), LDR 2514 (1.8%), Keladi 698 (0.5%), Jasmine 698 (0.5%). Outliers: soil moisture readings stuck at 0 or 100 or jumping 15-30 points for one minute (227 and 176); soil temperature -127 (sensor disconnected), 85 (DS18B20 reset) or 3-8 C jumps (128 and 156); DHT11 failed reads (air temperature below 1 C with humidity 0) and jumps (air temperature 114, humidity 115); LDR 0/100 or 30-50 point jumps (210). Label errors: 1397 Keladi and 1397 Jasmine values (1%) flipped at single random rows, as from relay or logging glitches. Outliers are also single cells at random rows.",
    "Days used": "Only calendar days with at least 60% real readings (864 of 1440 minutes) were kept: 88 indoor days, then 9 outdoor days. Kept days were placed back to back, so the dates run without gaps from 2026-05-12. The table below maps every new date to the original date. Days with less real data were dropped, not invented.",
    "Soil Temp, Env Temp, Humidity, LDR (base values)": "Based on the measured values: sensor faults removed, spikes removed with a 15-minute median filter, gaps filled from neighbouring days, 5-minute moving average, joins between non-adjacent days blended over 60 minutes. The day-to-day level of each column comes from the real readings (24-hour average). The noise and outliers above were added afterwards.",
    "Seasons (Penang)": "Each date follows approximate Penang (Bayan Lepas) monthly climate normals, interpolated day by day: mean air temperature (27.1 C in Nov-Dec to 28.4 C in Apr-May), humidity (74% in Jan-Feb to 84% in Oct), sunshine hours (8.2 h in Feb to 5.4 h in Sep), and sunrise/sunset times (about 07:06-07:28 / 19:02-19:36). Rainy days are drawn with the monthly rainy-day frequency; a rainy day is cooler, more humid and darker. Indoors only 15% of this effect is applied. 34 of 97 days are rainy. These normals are approximate values, not station data.",
    "Indoor lab days (controlled environment)": "Lights and air conditioning switch on and off at the times found in that day's real LDR log (58 of 88 lab days; the other 30 had none, mostly weekends). Never on between 19:00 and 07:00. With the AC on, humidity falls about 9%, air temperature about 0.6 C and soil temperature about 0.8 C, as in the real lab readings.",
    "Outdoor days": "Penang day/night cycle: dark from sunset to sunrise, LDR peak around 13:00, air temperature highest about 14:30 (+/-3.0 C), humidity the opposite way (+/-9.0%), soil temperature about 2 hours behind the air.",
    "Soil Moisture 1 and 2": "Simulated. Moisture falls continuously; the drying speed follows air temperature, humidity and light, averaging 14.0 points/day (Soil Moisture 1) and 10.0 points/day (Soil Moisture 2) indoors, 15.4 and 11.0 outdoors, with +/-20% day-to-day variation (real soil measured about 4.5 and 1.5 points/day). Pumped water reaches the sensor gradually (time constant 60 min), so moisture keeps rising for a while after pumping stops.",
    "Keladi, Jasmine (targets)": "1 = an irrigation event is in progress during that minute; 0 = no irrigation. Decisions are made on the noisy sensor reading, as the Arduino would. An event normally starts when the reading falls below a threshold that varies per event around 40 (37-43) and ends when it passes a stop level of 48-55 (4% of events stop early at 44-48), or when soil temperature <= 20 C, humidity >= 90 or air temperature > 35 C. About 30% of events start 1-20 minutes late and 3% of triggers are ignored for 30-120 minutes (pump fault, power cut). During an event the pump runs 3-second pulses with a 60 s soak, as in sketch_may3a.ino. A soil-moisture glitch can start or stop an event, as on the real device.",
    "Best Time (target)": "Target based on the time of collection. 1 = best time (20:00 to 04:59) or another allowed period (05:00 to 09:59 and 16:00 to 19:59, when the farmer is told it is not the best time to water). 0 = irrigation halted (10:00 to 15:59), the worst time because temperatures are highest. No blanks or errors in this column. 104760 rows = 1, 34920 rows = 0.",
    "Result": "Keladi: 160 irrigation events, 12695 rows = 1 (9.1%), peak moisture after irrigation 42 to 67, 3 skipped and 49 late starts. Jasmine: 121 events, 10977 rows = 1 (7.9%), peaks 47 to 64, 4 skipped and 45 late starts. Counts include the label errors and rows whose target is missing. Total rows: 139680.",
    "Not included": "Water Level and Distance. Raw readings remain in the original files.",
